# Newsletter Sign-Up Implementation

Here's a complete implementation with server-side validation, unsubscribe functionality, and explicit consent. I'll provide a Node.js/Express backend with PostgreSQL, but the patterns adapt to any stack.

## 1. Database Schema

```sql
CREATE TABLE subscribers (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  consent_given_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  unsubscribe_token TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  unsubscribed_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

-- Detect duplicate active subscriptions
CREATE INDEX idx_subscribers_email_active
  ON subscribers (email)
  WHERE unsubscribed_at IS NULL;
```

## 2. Backend (Node.js + Express)

```javascript
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const crypto = require('crypto');
const { Pool } = require('pg');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const app = express();
const pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });

app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));
app.use(express.json());

// RFC 5322-safe practical email regex
const EMAIL_RE = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]{2,}$/;

const signupLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 10,
  message: { error: 'Too many attempts. Please try again later.' }
});

// --- Sign-up ---
app.post('/api/newsletter/subscribe', signupLimiter, async (req, res) => {
  const email = String(req.body.email ?? '').trim().toLowerCase();
  const consent = req.body.consent === 'on' || req.body.consent === true;

  // 1. Server-side email validation
  if (email.length === 0 || email.length > 254 || !EMAIL_RE.test(email)) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Please enter a valid email address.' });
  }

  // 2. Explicit consent required (checkbox must be checked by user)
  if (!consent) {
    return res.status(400).json({
      error: 'Please tick the consent box to subscribe.'
    });
  }

  try {
    // Soft double opt-in: if subscriber exists but unsubscribed, reactivate
    const existing = await pool.query(
      'SELECT id, unsubscribed_at FROM subscribers WHERE email = $1',
      [email]
    );

    if (existing.rowCount > 0) {
      if (existing.rows[0].unsubscribed_at === null) {
        return res.status(200).json({
          message: 'You are already subscribed. Check your inbox.'
        });
      }
      const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');
      await pool.query(
        `UPDATE subscribers
         SET unsubscribed_at = NULL,
             consent_given_at = now(),
             unsubscribe_token = $2
         WHERE id = $1`,
        [existing.rows[0].id, token]
      );
    } else {
      const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');
      await pool.query(
        `INSERT INTO subscribers (email, consent_given_at, unsubscribe_token)
         VALUES ($1, now(), $2)`,
        [email, token]
      );
    }

    // TODO: send confirmation/welcome email (see note below)
    return res.status(201).json({
      message: 'Thanks — please check your inbox to confirm.'
    });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Something went wrong.' });
  }
});

// --- Unsubscribe ---
app.get('/newsletter/unsubscribe', async (req, res) => {
  const token = String(req.query.token ?? '');
  if (!/^[a-f0-9]{64}$/.test(token)) {
    return res.status(400).send('Invalid unsubscribe link.');
  }

  try {
    const result = await pool.query(
      `UPDATE subscribers
         SET unsubscribed_at = now()
       WHERE unsubscribe_token = $1
         AND unsubscribed_at IS NULL
       RETURNING id`,
      [token]
    );
    if (result.rowCount === 0) {
      return res.send('This link is invalid or you are already unsubscribed.');
    }
    res.send('You have been unsubscribed. Sorry to see you go!');
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    res.status(500).send('Something went wrong.');
  }
});

app.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000);
```

## 3. Frontend Form (HTML)

```html
<form id="newsletter-form" action="/api/newsletter/subscribe" method="post">
  <label for="email">Email address</label>
  <input
    type="email"
    id="email"
    name="email"
    required
    maxlength="254"
    autocomplete="email"
    placeholder="you@example.com"
  />

  <!-- Consent checkbox: NOT checked by default, required -->
  <label class="consent">
    <input
      type="checkbox"
      id="consent"
      name="consent"
      value="on"
      required
    />
    I agree to receive the newsletter and have read the
    <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </label>

  <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
  <p id="form-msg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
</form>

<script>
document.getElementById('newsletter-form').addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const form = e.currentTarget;
  const msg = document.getElementById('form-msg');
  msg.textContent = '';

  const res = await fetch(form.action, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: new URLSearchParams(new FormData(form))
  });
  const data = await res.json().catch(() => ({}));
  msg.textContent = data.message || data.error || 'Something went wrong.';
  if (res.ok) form.reset();
});
</script>
```

## 4. Unsubscribe email template (link)

```html
<p>
  <a href="https://yourdomain.com/newsletter/unsubscribe?token={{unsubscribe_token}}">
    Unsubscribe
  </a>
</p>
```

## Design notes & why this satisfies your requirements

**Server-side email validation.** The email is normalized (trimmed, lowercased) and validated with a regex on the server in addition to the client's `type="email"`. Server validation is the authoritative check; the browser one is a UX nicety. If you need stronger validation, add an SMTP deliverability check or use a library like `validator.js` — but be aware that deliverability checks can be abused for probing, so rate-limit them.

**Consent is active.** The checkbox has no `checked` attribute (never pre-checked), is `required` on the client, and the server **independently** rejects any request without `consent=on`. GDPR/ePrivacy guidance treats pre-ticked boxes as invalid consent, so both layers enforce an affirmative action. The `consent_given_at` timestamp gives you an audit trail. You should also log the IP and form version at time of consent if you need stronger evidentiary records.

**Unsubscribe works.** Every subscriber gets a random 64-char hex token (32 bytes from `crypto.randomBytes`). The unsubscribe endpoint accepts that token, marks `unsubscribed_at`, and is idempotent. Using tokens rather than raw emails in links means the email address never appears in URLs (which can leak via referrers/logs). The `WHERE unsubscribed_at IS NULL` guard means repeated clicks don't error.

**Consideration: double opt-in.** Real-world newsletters should send a confirmation email with a signed link before saving the subscriber as confirmed. I've noted where to add that; the schema supports it (add a `confirmed_at` column and treat unconfirmed rows as pending). This prevents subscription-bombing and gives you cleaner consent records.

**Other safeguards worth adding in production:**
- CSRF token on the form (if you move to cookies/sessions).
- A `List-Unsubscribe` and `List-Unsubscribe-Post` header on every outgoing email (required by Gmail/Yahoo bulk sender rules).
- Logging consent metadata (IP, user agent, timestamp, policy version) in a separate audit table.

Let me know your stack (Python/Django, Rails, PHP, etc.) if you'd like this translated.

